session21: narrate to the host stream, and prove the drop where the keys exist

Three harness defects, all found by running it.

1. `Note`/`Say` wrote to the PIPELINE, so any helper that both narrated and returned a
   value returned an array of its own notes. `$key = Find-SharedDrop ...` then held note
   strings, the phase probed `near:drop::0`, and the log said `no live actor matches
   'drop::0'` about a drop it had just found. session20.ps1 warns about exactly this trap
   for `Check`; the same trap was one level down. Narration now goes to the host stream
   and the report file, and the pipeline carries return values only.

2. A fixed 3 s read after writing qd.enemies lost the peer's dump -- see the probe. Every
   dump and drop probe now polls for its lines (60 s for a dump, 20 s for a resolver) and
   records how long it took.

3. The hub cannot prove loot hiding, and the run says why: a drop's key is
   {source_hash, drop_index} and the source is the enemy, so two machines running
   DIFFERENT spawner enemies have disjoint drop keys. Measured: three keys the host held
   and two the peer held, not one in common -- `rig: no live loot-ledger drop matches
   ... (the scan has 2 drop(s))` on the peer for every host key. The phase now enters
   780_SZ_Depths first, where both machines hold the same authored enemy keys, and kills
   through `qd.hit` (damage), because `qd.enemykill` runs the death but not the drop roll
   on the machine that calls it -- every drop of this session appeared on the OTHER
   machine.

Also: the readable-HP branch prints through `flt()` instead of a local CatFloat buffer,
which was producing `hp=1.00000000000000000` (right value, 17 decimals) on every line.

